      "keyInsight": "Most people treat influence like a mirror polishing contest. They think blending in makes them likable. In reality, our brains ignore perfectly smooth wallpaper. When you try to be flawless, people feel anxious around you. They sense an unwritten script. They keep their guard up. But when you own your odd quirks, magic happens. Imperfection acts like an open window in a stuffy room. It signals safety and zero hidden traps. Think of high-end sourdough bread. Nobody wants factory bread that looks like a blank white sponge. We crave the crackly crust, the uneven holes, and the sour punch. Your genuine oddities are your signature flavor. Trying to hide them turns you into generic elevator music. When you reveal your true colors, people relax. They lean in because you give them permission to be real too. Paradoxically, letting your odd flag fly makes you magnetic.",
