      "keyInsight": "Ego makes us think influence is a solo spotlight on a dark theater stage. If someone else steps into the beam, we panic that our face will fade into shadow. But influence is not a single desk lamp; it is a bonfire. When you hold a burning torch and touch it to a neighbor's dry wood, your flame does not shrink. The campsite just gets twice as bright. Insecure leaders hoard credit like dragons sitting on small piles of gold coins. They polish the glory, shut out their peers, and wonder why nobody cheers for their victories. When you actively pull teammates into the light and praise their craft, you do not disappear. You become the master director everyone wants to perform with. You gain the rarest persuasion power on earth: a crowd of skilled people who trust your hand.",
      "content": "Think about a seasoned kitchen chef running a busy dinner rush. If the head chef runs out to the dining room and boasts that he peeled every potato, chopped every onion, and stirred every soup alone, the guests laugh. Everyone knows a great dinner is a symphony of skilled hands. When the chef walks out and applauds the prep cooks and sauce makers, the entire restaurant cheers louder. Sharing credit makes the leader look taller, not smaller.\n\nToo many people hoard praise like desert travelers hoarding water bottles. They rush into meetings to claim every creative spark. But people possess built-in antennas for vanity. When you hog the stage, your colleagues stop bringing you their best ideas. Why hand a gem to someone who will hide it in their own pocket? Hoarding credit quietly starves your own influence.\n\nInstead, train yourself to be a spotlight operator. When an idea succeeds, point out the quiet colleague who cracked the hard problem. Celebrate your partner's sharp thinking in front of senior leaders. People remember who made them feel respected and capable. When you push others up the podium, they will gladly carry you across the finish line when the road gets steep.",
